proof:
The set $\{(z,x):0\le z\le3,\ x\in(94+3z)T/100\}$ is the convex hull of its extreme fibers because the radius is affine and positive and Minkowski combinations of nonnegative homothets of one convex set add their scales. It is full dimensional and has the four stated integer fibers.

After dividing the target by $\Gamma V/2$ and clearing positive denominators, the first comparison follows from $\lambda\le4\Gamma^2$ and the first integer below. The second comparison is precisely the positivity of the second integer:
$$
\begin{aligned}
21^{20}(94^{20}+97^{20}-100^{20}+103^{20})-8\cdot20^{20}97^{20}
 &=1137549378322964594658090728038214421874179602838935201279638978,\\
94^{20}+97^{20}+100^{20}-103^{20}
 &=277893493887952690657232019960683602976.
\end{aligned}
$$
The accepted weighted-tail proof 0609de1167223ac2 gives $\lambda_{20}\le4\Gamma^2$ by arithmetic-geometric mean, for the same coefficient as 4b772ae7fa6b3293. To see the actual single-middle certificate, subtract the affine centroid graph $r(z)g_T$ from the continuous coordinates. At bases $1,2,3$ the centered fibers become $r_1(T-g_T),T-g_T,r_3(T-g_T)$. The first is contained in the second, and the third contains a copy of the second. Apply 4b772ae7fa6b3293 to the retained subsystem with the two full copies of $T-g_T$ and subset $r_1(T-g_T)$, whose volume is $Vr_1^{20}$. Adding discarded mass cannot reduce the original raw payment. At base $3$ the exposing tilt and ordinary simplex maximum of 1a16e89734914fc8 §6 give exactly $\Gamma V r_3^{20}$ as the best own-fiber depth.

For the actual mark at base $2$, the fixed-mark affine-selector calculation of bb2b5f9519ae23e8 applies to the centroid graph $z\mapsto r(z)g_T$ with common ordinary depth fraction $\Gamma$, by 1a16e89734914fc8 §6. A through-mark cap contains the graph over at least one of the two closed tails through $2$. Their volume sums divided by $V$ are $r_0^{20}+r_1^{20}+1$ and $1+r_3^{20}$. The first exceeds half the total by the second integer above. The second exceeds half by $1>r_0^{20}$ and $r_3^{20}>r_1^{20}$. This yields the strict actual depth lower bound for every closed containing cap.
